"We start with the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2x - 3y = 8 \\\\\n4x + 3y = -2\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we can simplify the second equation by dividing all terms by 3:\n\\[\n4x + 3y = -2 \\implies 2x + y = -2\n\\]\n\nNow, we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2x - 3y = 8 \\\\\n2x + y = -2\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add the two equations together to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(2x - 3y) + (2x + y) = 8 + (-2)\n\\]\n\\[\n4x = 6\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(x = 1\\) back into the second equation:\n\\[\n2(1) + y = -2\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -4\n\\]\n\nThe product of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) \\(xy\\) is \\(1 \\cdot (-4) = \\boxed{-4}\\)."
"To solve the equation \\( |2 - |x|| = 1 \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The equation \\( |2 - |x|| = 1 \\) can be split into two separate equations:\n\n1. \\( 2 - |x| = 1 \\)\n2. \\( 2 - |x| = -1 \\)\n\nLet's solve each equation separately:\n\n1. \\( 2 - |x| = 1 \\)\n   \\[\n   |x| = 1\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( x = \\pm 1 \\).\n\n2. \\( 2 - |x| = -1 \\)\n   \\[\n   |x| = 3\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( x = \\pm 3 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of the squares of all real values of \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n(1 + (-1))^2 + (3 + (-3))^2 = 1 + 9 + 9 = 19\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the squares of all real values of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{19}\\)."
"First, we factor the denominator on the left-hand side:\n\\[x^2 - 8x + 15 = (x - 3)(x - 5).\\]\n\nNext, we combine the right-hand side over a common denominator:\n\\[\\frac{A}{x-3} + \\frac{B}{x-5} = \\frac{A(x-5) + B(x-3)}{(x-3)(x-5)}.\\]\n\nEquating the numerators, we get:\n\\[4x = A(x-5) + B(x-3).\\]\n\nExpanding and combining like terms, we have:\n\\[4x = (A+B)x - (5A - 3B).\\]\n\nFor the equation to hold for all $x$, the coefficients of $x$ and the constant terms must be equal on both sides:\n\\[A + B = 0,\\]\n\\[-5A - 3B = 4.\\]\n\nSolving these equations, we find:\n\\[A = 2,\\]\n\\[B = -2.\\]\n\nThus, the solution $(A, B) = \\boxed{(2, -2)}.$"
"Let the first term of the geometric sequence be \\(a\\) and the common ratio be \\(r\\). The second term is \\(ar = -2\\) and the fifth term is \\(ar^4 = 16\\). We can find the fourteenth term, which is \\(ar^{13}\\).\n\nFirst, we solve for \\(r\\) using the second term:\n\\[ar = -2\\]\n\\[r = \\frac{-2}{a}\\]\n\nNext, we use the fifth term:\n\\[ar^4 = 16\\]\n\\[a \\left(\\frac{-2}{a}\\right)^4 = 16\\]\n\\[a \\left(\\frac{16}{a^4}\\right) = 16\\]\n\\[a^4 = 16\\]\n\\[a = 2\\]\n\nNow, we find the fourteenth term:\n\\[ar^{13} = 2 \\left(\\frac{-2}{2}\\right)^{13} = 2(-2)^{13} = 2(-8192) = -6384\\]\n\nTherefore, the fourteenth term is \\(\\boxed{-6384}\\)."
