"To find the value of \\( h(-1) \\) for the function \\( h(x) = \\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{2}} \\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute \\( x = -1 \\) into the function.\n2. Simplify the expression inside the square root.\n3. Take the square root of the simplified expression.\n\nLet's go through these steps in detail:\n\n1. Substitute \\( x = -1 \\) into the function:\n   \\[\n   h(-1) = \\sqrt{\\frac{-1 + 3}{2}}\n   \\]\n\n2. Simplify the expression inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   -1 + 3 = 2\n   \\]\n   So the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2}{2} = 1\n   \\]\n\n3. Take the square root of the simplified expression:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{1} = 1\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( h(-1) \\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $1$. I hope it is correct."
