"Given the function \\( h(x) = ax + b \\) and the conditions \\( h(1) = 5 \\) and \\( h(-1) = 1 \\), we can set up the following equations:\n\n1. \\( h(1) = a(1) + b = a + b = 5 \\)\n2. \\( h(-1) = a(-1) + b = -a + b = 1 \\)\n\nWe can solve these equations simultaneously. Adding the two equations:\n\n\\[\n(a + b) + (-a + b) = 5 + 1 \\implies 2a = 6 \\implies a = 3\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( a = 3 \\) back into the first equation:\n\n\\[\n3 + b = 5 \\implies b = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the function \\( h(x) = 3x + 2 \\). We need to find \\( h(6) \\):\n\n\\[\nh(6) = 3(6) + 2 = 18 + 2 = 20\n\\]\n\nThe value of \\( h(6) \\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."
"First, we combine the square roots:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{60x} \\cdot \\sqrt{12x} \\cdot \\sqrt{63x} = \\sqrt{60x \\cdot 12x \\cdot 63x}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the expression inside the square root:\n\\[\n60x \\cdot 12x \\cdot 63x = 60 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 63 \\cdot x^3\n\\]\n\nCalculate the product of the constants:\n\\[\n60 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 63 = 45360\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n45360x^3 \n\\]\n\nNow, we take the square root of the expression:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{45360x^3} = \\sqrt{45360} \\cdot \\sqrt{x^3} = 210 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sqrt{x}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final simplified form in simplest radical form is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{210x\\sqrt{x}}\n\\]"
"First, we calculate the amount after six months in the first CD. The annual interest rate is $4\\%$, compounded semi-annually, so the semi-annual interest rate is $4\\% / 2 = 2\\%$. The amount after six months is:\n\n$$10000 \\left(1 + 0.02\\right)^2 = 10000 \\left(1.02\\right)^2 = 10000 \\times 1.0404 = 10404.$$\n\nNext, we calculate the amount after six months in the second CD. The annual interest rate is $5\\%$, compounded semi-annually, so the semi-annual interest rate is $5\\% / 2 = 2.5\\%$. The amount after six months is:\n\n$$10404 \\left(1 + 0.025\\right)^2 = 10404 \\left(1.025\\right)^2 = 10404 \\times 1.050625 = \\boxed{10922.5}.$$\n\nI hope it is correct."
"To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{1}{5}|9+2a|< <\\), we start by isolating the absolute value expression. Multiply both sides of the inequality by 5 to eliminate the fraction:\n\n\\[\n5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}|9+2a| < 5 \\cdot 1\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n|9+2a| < 5\n\\]\n\nThe absolute value inequality \\(|9+2a| < 5\\) can be split into two separate inequalities:\n\n\\[\n9 + 2a < 5\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n9 + 2a > -5\n\\]\n\nSolving the first inequality:\n\n\\[\n9 + 2a < 5 \\implies 2a < -4 \\implies a < -2\n\\]\n\nSolving the second inequality:\n\n\\[\n9 + 2a > -5 \\implies 2a > -14 \\implies a > -7\n\\]\n\nCombining these two results, we find that \\(a\\) must satisfy both conditions in the interval:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-7, -2)}\n\\]"
