"To solve the equation \\(|4x+2|=10\\) given that \\(x<0\\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value function \\(|A|\\) is defined as:\n\n\\[ |A| = \\begin{cases} \nA & \\text{if } A \\geq 0 \\\\\n-A & \\text{if } A < 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nGiven that \\(x < 0\\), the expression \\(4x + 2\\) will be negative. Therefore, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[ -(4x + 2) = 10 \\]\n\nNow, we solve this equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[ -4x - 2 = 10 \\]\n\nAdd 2 to both sides:\n\n\\[ -4x = 12 \\]\n\nDivide both sides by -4:\n\n\\[ x = -3 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation given \\(x < 0\\) is \\(\\boxed{-3}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(-3\\). I hope it is correct."
"First, we need to evaluate \\(\\lfloor 11.1 \\rfloor\\). The floor function \\(\\lfloor x \\rfloor\\) gives the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(x\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\lfloor 11.1 \\rfloor = 11\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to evaluate \\(\\lfloor 0.5 \\cdot 11.1 \\rfloor\\). First, calculate \\(0.5 \\cdot 11.1\\):\n\n\\[\n0.5 \\cdot 11.1 = 5.55\n\\]\n\nNow, find the floor of \\(5.55\\):\n\n\\[\n\\lfloor 5.55 \\rfloor = 5\n\\]\n\nNow, we multiply this result by 2:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 5 = 10\n\\]\n\nFinally, we add the two results together:\n\n\\[\n\\lfloor 11.1 \\rfloor + 2 \\cdot \\lfloor 0.5 \\cdot 11.1 \\rfloor = 11 + 10 = 21\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxedboxed21}\\)."
"To determine the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{(2x-3)(2x+5)}{(3x-9)(3x+6)} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. The function is undefined where the denominator is zero.\n\nFirst, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n3x - 9 = 0 \\implies x = 3\n\\]\n\\[\n3x + 6 = 0 \\implies x = -2\n\\]\n\nThus, the function \\( f(x) \\) is not defined at \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( x = 3 \\). Therefore, the domain of \\( f(x all real numbers except \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( x = 3 \\).\n\nIn interval notation, the domain is:\n\n\\[\n(-\\infty, -2) \\cup (-2, 3) \\cup (3, \\infty)\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\((-\\infty, -2) \\cup (-2, 3) \\cup (3, \\infty)\\). I hope it is correct."
"To find the minimum possible slope of the line, we need to consider the fact that the line passes through the point \\((4, 365)\\) and the \\(y\\)-intercept \\(b\\) is between 1 and 9. The slope \\(m\\) of a line passing through a point \\((4, 365)\\) and a \\(y\\)-intercept \\(b\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{365 - b}{4} \\]\n\nTo minimize the slope \\(m\\), we need to maximize the numerator \\(365 - b\\). Since \\(b\\) is between 1 and 9, the maximum value of \\(b\\) is 9. Therefore, the maximum value of \\(365 - b\\) is:\n\n\\[ 365 - 9 = 356 \\]\n\nNow, we substitute this back into the slope formula:\n\n\\[ m = \\frac{356}{4} = 89 \\]\n\nThus, the minimum possible slope of the line is \\(89\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{89}\n\\]"
"To find the amount in Alan's bank account after 10 years with an annual interest rate of 3%, we can use the formula for compound interest:\n\n\\[ A = P \\times (1 + r)^n \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( P \\) is the amount of money (500),\n- \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (3% or 0.03),\n- \\( n \\) is the number of years (10).\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\n\\[ A = 500 \\times \\left( 1 + 0.03 \\right)^{10} \\]\n\n\\[ A = 500 \\times \\left( 1.03 \\right)^{10} \\]\n\n\\[ A \\approx 500 \\times 1.343916 \\]\n\n\\[ A \\approx 671.958 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest dollar, the amount in Alan's bank account after 10 years is \\boxed{672}.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $672. I hope it is correct."
