"To compute \\(\\cos 45^\\circ\\), we can use the definition of cosine in a right triangle. The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nConsider a right triangle where one of the angles is \\(45^\\circ\\). In such a triangle, the two legs are of equal length, and the hypotenuse is \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) times the length of each leg.\n\nLet's denote the length of each leg by \\(x\\). Then, the length of the hypotenuse is \\(x\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nThe cosine of \\(45^\\circ\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\cos 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\cos 45^\\circ\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\).\n\n---\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\). I hope it is correct."
