"To find the perimeter of \\(\\triangle PQS\\), we need to sum the lengths of all three sides. From the diagram, we know the lengths of two sides:\n\n- \\(PQ = 13\\)\n- \\(QS = 37\\)\n\nWe also know that \\(QR = 5\\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the length of \\(PS\\). Since \\(\\triangle PQS\\) is a right triangle with \\(QS\\) as the hypotenuse, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\(PS\\):\n\n\\[\nPS = \\sqrt{PQ^2 + QR^2} = \\sqrt{13^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{169 + 25} = \\sqrt{194} = 14\n\\]\n\nNow, we have the lengths of all three sides of the triangle. The perimeter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle PQS\\) is the sum of these lengths:\n\n\\[\nP = PQ + QS + PS = 13 + 37 + 14 = 64\n\\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of \\(\\triangle PQS\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\)."
